dy being built  german company siemens is installing new  trains on the line the trains and their  tracks have all been checked by  germany's safety organization toof  on the yellow metro line only modern  trains are in service  they're seen as a big improvement  the first important thing to say about  this line is that it's driverless it  doesn't need a driver the system is  operated completely by computer and  other electronic systems that's the  biggest difference with this line there  are also other differences such as an  end-to-end aisle as you can see here  also the doors on the station open  simultaneously with the train doors for  safety reasons  this is the hotel unique in pineros  outside lincoln paiva and his co-worker  zhao amaral have found a novel way  around sao paulo's never-ending traffic  jams  they've come up with something never  seen before in brazil  a sightseeing tour by bicycle  today's tour will last two hours and  take these sightseers through the villa  madalena sao paulo's artistic and  bohemian quarter  the group's first stop is the museo  brasileiro de escultura an open air  museum showcasing alternative and  contemporary art  the museum has no doors so there's time  for a quick look around  with much more to see the group sets off  for the next stop on their tour  a well-hidden attraction in sao paulo is  the becco samsaida a dead-end street  adorned with graffiti  there are others like it dotted around  the city  the tour guide says the best way to see  them is by bicycle  shortly if you don't have a life or a  routine where you use the car every day  and you you stay at home of course you  get a lot of weight and people say well  instead of using the bicycle to lose  that weight they keep on doing keeping  their routine on the car as to gain even  more weight so of course it all you  always have to begin and it's always  going to be tougher in the beginning but  then you're going to see the results  we bring it here especially to show the  culture of the street art and show uh  one of the best characteristics here in  uh villa madalena which is that some  hidden places uh in sao paulo that  people normally don't know and that they  don't even have an idea that this  place exists  what makes the villa madalena so special  is that it grew naturally unlike other  towns and cities in brazil  there are a lot of small unique shops  here  shops like this one  inside it's like taking a trip back in  time to the 1950s  it's another stop on the tour that  lincoln paiva is proud of  at first the idea had nothing to do with  the world cup the idea was to create a  sustainable means of transport for sao  paulo  and for other cities in general we  wanted to offer an alternative so that  people use their cars less  but we also want to use the world cup in  the olympic games just as other cities  across the world have used them to  improve our transportation network  germany is a good example of that  the tour rounds off with a visit to a  tea house it's a great place for tea  connoisseurs  there are more than three dozen  different blends on offer here all of  them grown organically  it's the perfect end to a sportier kind  of tour  in pakayambu stadium the fans of the  corinthians palista football club are  warming up for their showdown with arch  rival sao kaitano  brazilian football fans are unrivaled in  their love and passion for the game but  that can sometimes make them difficult  to control  the stadium here dates back to 1938 and  is a long way off the stringent  standards required to host a world cup  game  but this is where the tournament's  opening match will be kicking off so the  pachaimbu stadium will have to be  completely renovated  that's as long as authorities don't  decide on a new stadium instead  football fans here don't think the  tournament will be a success  i'm the kind of guy who believes in many  things but i doubt we'll be able to  stage a world cup on a par with the one  in germany germany is a first world  country and everything there was a  hundred percent safety transport  everything every stadium could be  reached with the metro i look around  here and think we can't have the world  cup here when i consider what i saw in  germany  at least for us here in brazil football  is everything football is love it's  happiness it's religion football makes  football fans happy  over a thousand kilometers to the  southwest in puerto alegre two teams are  battling it out in a penalty shootout  the league title is at stake and it's  brazilians high flyers international who  take the glory  patrick dauda reflects on his time in  brazil  i really like brazil there's just a  great atmosphere here people have lots  of enthusiasm and i've already seen some  of that they're motivated and i think  they'll manage to get everything ready  in time for the world cup there are  still a couple of technical things left  to do but i'm sure they'll manage  everything in any case it's a great  country with great people and i really  enjoyed it  wow  you
 

